**定理 9.1（classification reused at its actual scope）。** In addition to the standing domain, put
$D_{\rm crit}=L^{2/\varphi^2}$. The ten ordinary PCR regimes remain:

| Row | Parameter condition | Entire-future class |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | $L<1$, any $H>0$ | Linear contraction |
| 2 | $L=1,\ H<1$ | Linear contraction |
| 3 | $L=1,\ H\ge1$ | Unit plateau, with neutral members |
| 4 | $L>1,\ d_0<D_{\rm crit},\ H<1$ | Linear contraction |
| 5 | $L>1,\ d_0<D_{\rm crit},\ H=1$ | Unit plateau; neutral members exactly when $d_0\le1$ |
| 6 | $L>1,\ d_0<D_{\rm crit},\ H>1$ | Growth |
| 7 | $L>1,\ d_0=D_{\rm crit},\ H\le1$ | Zero future |
| 8 | $L>1,\ d_0=D_{\rm crit},\ H>1$ | Growth |
| 9 | $L>1,\ d_0>D_{\rm crit},\ H<H_\star$ | Zero future |
| 10 | $L>1,\ d_0>D_{\rm crit},\ H\ge H_\star$ | Growth, including attained equality |

There are exactly three linear rows, 1,2,4. These are the original full-real-class future/modulus regimes, not assertions that every source in a growth row grows. The finite interpreter of §§3–7 is used at its unit-read contracting scope. The strict-error optimal law is only the neutral-free subcase of row 5 stated in Theorem 6.3.

Precisely, for actual pairs define $d_3=\max_{0\le i\le2}\|x_i-x_i'\|$,
$d_\infty=\sup_{j\ge3}\|x_j-x_j'\|$ and
$\Omega(\eta)=\sup_{d_3\le\eta}d_\infty$. In every row $\Omega(0)=0$. In rows 1,2,4, $\Omega(\eta)=\Theta(\eta)$ for small positive $\eta$; in rows 7,9 it is zero for all $\eta$; in rows 6,8,10 it is infinite for every positive $\eta$. On the two plateaus, $\Omega(\eta)=1$ for sufficiently small positive $\eta$, $\Omega(\eta)\le2$, and $\Omega(\eta)=2$ for $\eta\ge2$. No complete intermediate plateau or sharp-noise curve is asserted.

**证明（exact reuse and equality conditions）。** PCR §§18–19,24–28,53 prove the source support, joint feasibility and regime classification. The load-bearing correspondence is as follows. Nonzero-label support is

$$
\mathcal S=\{(1,0),(0,1)\}\ \cup\
\{(m,n):m,n>0,\ (m,n)\notin(2\mathbb N)^2\}.
$$

The three unit axes have the nonzero $\mathbb F_2^2$ grades; a both-even composition has zero grade, and a pure multi-leaf tree contains a zero cherry. Conversely, starting with $\langle\beta,\alpha\rangle$, two left grafts of one leaf type add two counts without killing the unit label. An optional single left graft produces either remaining parity. This realizes every supported composition; reversing the root realizes the opposite sign.

Orthogonalization preserves $\|b\|$ and $\Delta$, and projection in Lemma 2.1 lowers the initial norm to the joint orthogonal minimum; scaling both leaves then lowers all three norms to $\Delta=d_0$. Let $z=\log\sqrt{d_0}$ and $\ell=\log L$. At $v=\log r\in[z-\ell,\ell]$ the three simultaneous logarithmic read norms are

$$
f_0=mz+(n-m)v,\qquad f_1=nz+mv,\qquad f_2=f_0+f_1 .
$$

They are achieved by one supported tree and the one orthogonal preparation
$(e^{z-v}e_1,e^ve_2)$. Marginal minimizers from different preparations are never combined.
The future Perron coefficient is
$(m+\varphi n)(v+\varphi z)$. Its sign follows from the exact Fibonacci solution for the logarithmic recurrence. At
$G=\varphi^2z-\ell=0$, the endpoint read slopes use
$n-\varphi m\ne0$ and their maximum is strictly positive, although Fibonacci compositions approach zero. Thus the nonzero read-bound infimum is one and is not attained; row 7 includes equality.

For $G>0$ the attained threshold is exactly

$$
\log H_\star=\min_{(m,n)\in\mathcal S}
\ \min_{v\in[z-\ell,\ell]}\max(f_0,f_1,f_2).
$$

The single $\alpha$ leaf gives $H_\star\le\sqrt{d_0}$. Since
$f_1+\varphi f_2=(m+\varphi n)(v+\varphi z)\ge(m+\varphi n)G$,
any composition that can improve or tie this leaf upper bound obeys
$m+\varphi n\le\varphi^2z/G$. There are only finitely many such compositions; the maximum of three affine functions attains its minimum on each closed interval. Hence $1<H_\star\le\sqrt{d_0}$ is achieved by a genuine tree/preparation, and its positive Perron coefficient gives growth at equality in row 10. For $G<0$ the existing supported contraction and growth families in PCR §§25–28 give rows 4–6 with the stated read-bound splits. For $L\le1$, the nonempty-tree leaf bound and those same recurrence estimates give rows 1–3. This uses the established joint classification, not a new extremum calculation.

The exact example $H_\star(6,4)=32/27$ retains its PCR §31 finite-coverage proof and attained composition $(3,5)$; no new enumeration is asserted. Its eight-leaf right-grafted tree and preparation $(6e_1,e_2/3)$ have simultaneous vectors

$$
x_0=(0,0,8/9),\quad x_1=(-32/27,0,0),\quad
x_2=(0,-256/243,0),\quad x_3=(0,0,8192/6561).
$$

In the linear rows an effective implementation uses separately represented true rational contraction bounds, not a decision of inequalities between arbitrary real parameters. The constant-gap versions of the finite head/whole-tail constructions in PCR §§52,68,73 apply, or §7 applies to their unit histories. In the zero rows a truthful zero-future certificate permits the exact zero core. Its acquisition, envelope and evidence are still paid. Proper rotations of a nonzero growing member give the full-real-class unbounded-error obstructions retained in PCR §§25,57,65.

